<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ma'an</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ma'an is a city in southern Jordan on the old caravan and pilgrimage route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, later served by the Hejaz Railway. In November 1920 Abdullah, son of Sharif Husayn of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, arrived there with his followers, the first step towards founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Transjordan. It is the capital of the Ma'an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, which includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maan-jordan.jpg" alt="Ma'an" width="320" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: American Colony (Jerusalem). Photo Dept. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petra-Locomotive.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ma'an lies in southern Jordan, 218 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a>, at a height above 1,000 metres. The climate is a cold desert one, with hot summers, mild to cold winters and rainfall averaging only 44 millimetres a year. The modern city stands just north-west of the ancient town of the same name, which dates at least to the Nabatean period. It lies on the old King's Highway and on the modern Desert Highway, and it is the capital of the Ma'an Governorate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ma'an was founded, according to the account given for it, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-main">Minaeans</a>, an ancient Arab people based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, between the 2nd and 4th century BCE, on a major trade route. Local tradition holds that it was named after Ma'an, the son of Lot. Under Byzantine rule it lay in the territory of the Arab Christian tribe of Banu Judham. Its governor in the early 7th century, Farwa ibn Amr al-Judhami, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and was executed by crucifixion on the Byzantine emperor's order. The Muslim state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> then sent an army under Usama ibn Zayd to conquer the town.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> ran through Ma'an, and its economy depended on the pilgrim caravans. Its main trade partner was Gaza, and it sold livestock, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and sheep, to pilgrims. Most residents were literate and many served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> or advisers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a>, with whom the town had unusually good relations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> annexed Transjordan in 1517 and in 1559 built a fort to protect the pilgrim road. In the late nineteenth century they tried several times to raise Ma'an's rank, but al-Karak was preferred as district capital. The railway station opened in 1902, connecting the city to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><p>In April 1918 the Arab Northern Army besieged the Ottoman garrison at the Battle of Maan, from 13 to 17 April. It failed to take the city but wrecked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> railway to the south. In 1920 the Hashemite emir Abdullah I arrived with several hundred fighters in an attempt to restore the Sharifian throne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> after its overthrow by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Ma'an was annexed to the Transjordanian state in 1925.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ma'an is the seat of Al-Hussein Bin Talal University and of the Ma'an Development Area, a special economic zone whose industrial park began construction in 2008. A railway building at the station, used by Abdullah I as the seat of the first government, is now called the Ma'an Palace and appears on the reverse of the 5 dinar note. Most of the inhabitants are Sunni Muslims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ma'an” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
